I have enough to synthesize. Findings below are read-only observations; I could not run the gate.

FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:442-449 (and BENCH.md:3,7-9) — the resumed change now writes wall-clock time into the *tracked* `BENCH.md`, and `render_markdown` emits `row.elapsed` plus the machine name, so every `rb-bench` run rewrites a tracked file with different numbers. This is wall-clock nondeterminism in a checked-in artifact: the "baseline" is not reproducible across machines/builds, and nothing anywhere compares a future run against these values, so the report's claim that "a later phase can detect a regression" is unsupported. It also directly contradicts `render_timings`' own doc at tests/bench/mod.rs:488-493 ("Machine-specific and not checked in") and the new `.gitignore` comment that "only the verified answers are tracked". Concrete fix: keep volatile timings in the untracked `BENCH_TIMINGS.md` and record only verified answers in `BENCH.md`, or add an explicit hardware/build-normalisation story plus a comparison tool; at minimum update the `.gitignore`/`render_timings` prose so the repo has one contract. Not run-verified; traced by reading the render path and the docs.

2. [MAJOR] tests/bench_test.rs:27-29 and 684-686 — both baseline tests locate the checked-in file via `bench::bench_md_path()` (tests/bench/mod.rs:534-537), which returns `$RB_BENCH_OUTPUT` when set. That override is a documented, supported mode, so in any environment that sets it these tests read a foreign/stale file instead of the repo's `BENCH.md`; the checked-in baseline is then never validated (or the test fails spuriously). The sibling test at tests/bench_test.rs:284-293 already guards `if RB_BENCH_OUTPUT.is_none()`, which shows the hazard is known. Fix: resolve the baseline from the manifest directory, as `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:450-452` does with `env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")`, instead of ambient cwd/env. This is an env-coupled test defect.

3. [MAJOR] tests/bench_test.rs:45-48 — this diff inverts an existing assertion from `!text.contains("Wall time")` ("the checked-in baseline must not record volatile wall times") to `text.contains("Wall time")`. That removes the only guard that kept volatile timings out of the tracked baseline and replaces it with an assertion demanding exactly the property the previous test forbade. Combined with finding 1, the repository now contains two contradictory contracts (the test demands tracked timings; `.gitignore`/`render_timings` say timings are untracked). Fix: decide the contract and make the test and the documentation agree; if timings stay in `BENCH.md`, delete the contradictory `.gitignore`/`render_timings` wording and state how cross-machine comparison works.

4. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:596-606 — the no-argument runner writes `BENCH.md` first and only then writes `BENCH_TIMINGS.md`; if the second write fails it returns exit 1 after the tracked baseline has already been replaced, leaving a half-updated output set. Fix: write both to temporary paths and rename only after both succeed, or write the timing artifact first.

5. [MINOR] tests/bench/mod.rs:511-526 — `hardware_note()` reads `/proc/cpuinfo` and silently falls back to "unknown CPU" on non-Linux, yet `BENCH.md` claims to name the machine the timings were measured on. On macOS/Windows the tracked baseline's hardware line becomes meaningless. Fix: use a platform-agnostic probe or make the "unknown CPU" fallback explicit in the file's prose.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 5
